WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.840
Is a trout more closely related to a shark, or to you?

2
00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.180
The two fish look alike.

3
00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:12.100
A tree gives a different answer.

4
00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:16.913
A phylogenetic tree is a hypothesis about ancestry.

5
00:00:18.273 --> 00:00:19.993
The living species sit at the tips.

6
00:00:21.333 --> 00:00:24.513
Here are five: lamprey, shark, trout, frog and human.

7
00:00:25.833 --> 00:00:27.593
Nobody watched these lineages split.

8
00:00:28.833 --> 00:00:33.733
The tree is estimated from characters, usually DNA or protein sequences.

9
00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:37.927
Each point where a branch splits is an internal node.

10
00:00:39.187 --> 00:00:43.407
It stands for an inferred ancestor of every lineage descending from it.

11
00:00:44.487 --> 00:00:48.327
The root, on the left, is the ancestor of everything on the tree.

12
00:00:49.417 --> 00:00:53.057
Lamprey is the outgroup, assumed to lie outside the rest.

13
00:00:54.357 --> 00:00:56.157
The root tells you which end is older.

14
00:00:57.417 --> 00:01:01.117
So for two nodes on one lineage, you can say which is more recent.

15
00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:05.780
Now trace trout and shark back until their lines meet.

16
00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:10.800
They meet at node J. Then trace trout and human.

17
00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:13.120
They meet at node B.

18
00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:18.130
Node B descends from node J, so B is more recent.

19
00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:23.010
B is the most recent common ancestor of trout and human.

20
00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.610
That is the latest node from which both descend.

21
00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:29.450
Shark does not descend from B.

22
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:34.040
So a trout is more closely related to you than to a shark.

23
00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:36.880
It is equally close to the frog.

24
00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.740
A clade is an ancestor and all its descendants.

25
00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:44.340
Trout, frog and human form one clade.

26
00:01:45.383 --> 00:01:48.503
Now spin node T, like one arm of a mobile.

27
00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:51.983
Frog and human swap places on the page.

28
00:01:53.243 --> 00:01:57.243
Every clade keeps its members, so nothing about the history has changed.

29
00:01:58.317 --> 00:02:03.637
Now spin node B. Shark will no longer sit beside trout on the page.

30
00:02:04.897 --> 00:02:06.637
Does trout's closest relative change?

31
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.420
No. The drawing changed, but the tree did not.

32
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:24.760
Trout still meets frog and human at node B, and shark at node J. Tips side by side

33
00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:28.220
on the page are not, for that reason, closest relatives.

34
00:02:29.383 --> 00:02:30.723
One more misreading.

35
00:02:32.323 --> 00:02:34.523
Human sits at the end, so is it more evolved?

36
00:02:35.903 --> 00:02:40.443
No. Every living tip has had the same time since the root.

37
00:02:41.983 --> 00:02:45.243
Lampreys lack jaws, but they have derived features of their own.

38
00:02:46.267 --> 00:02:50.567
So characters, not whole organisms, are ancestral or derived.

39
00:02:51.827 --> 00:02:54.147
And relatedness is read only at the nodes.

40
00:02:55.467 --> 00:03:00.067
Find the most recent common ancestor, and ignore how the drawing happens to look.
